.TH CHTTPCLIENT_DO 3 "2026-07-11" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpclient_do \- perform an HTTP request and buffer the full response
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <chttpclient.h>

ccol_retval_t chttpclient_do(chttpcli cli,
                             const chttp_request_t *req,
                             chttpcli_response **resp_out);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Acquires a pool slot (blocking if all slots are in use), executes the
HTTP request synchronously, buffers the entire response body, and
stores a heap-allocated chttpcli_response in *resp_out.

*resp_out is set to NULL immediately, before any other work begins,
and stays NULL on every non-success return; it is safe to
unconditionally call chttpclient_resp_free(resp) after this call
regardless of the returned ccol_retval_t, with no need to separately
pre-initialize the caller's own local pointer. The caller owns
*resp_out on success and must call chttpclient_resp_free when done.

Redirects (301, 302, 303, 307, 308) are followed automatically, up to
50 hops.  301/302/303 rewrite the method to a bodyless GET (HEAD is
left as HEAD); 307/308 preserve the original method and body.  The
Location header may be an absolute URL, a protocol-relative
reference ("//host/path"), an absolute-path reference ("/foo"), or a
general relative reference ("foo", "../foo", "./foo", "?query");
all are resolved per RFC 3986.  A Location value that carries its
own scheme (e.g. "mailto:x@y", "ftp://host/path", or any scheme
other than http, https, or http+unix) is always treated as absolute
per RFC 3986 SS5.2.2 and resolves to itself unchanged; since this
client only connects over http/https/http+unix, the next hop then
reports ccol_http_invalid_url (the same code an unsupported scheme
in the original request URL already gets) instead of the reference
being silently merged onto the current origin's path as though it
were relative.  If the 50-hop cap is reached and the
last hop's response is itself a would-be redirect, it is not
followed or delivered; ccol_http_too_many_redirects is returned
instead.  Dot-segment normalization
("..", ".") only ever rewrites the path component; a query string
is always carried forward byte-for-byte, even one that happens to
contain "/", "..", or "." characters.  If the request URL embedded
credentials (see below), the resulting Authorization header is
resent on every hop that stays on the same origin (scheme, host,
and port) and is dropped permanently the first time a hop changes
origin.  An Authorization header set explicitly via
chttp_request_set_header receives the identical treatment, tracked
against the origin of the original request rather than any per-hop
userinfo (matching curl's own hardened default behavior of never
forwarding a user-set Authorization header to a different host on
redirect).

A 301/302/303 hop that rewrites the method to a bodyless GET also
strips any caller-set Content-Length, Content-Type, and Expect
header from that hop and every hop after it (unless a later 307/308
hop restores a real body): those headers only ever describe a body,
and resending a value that described the original request's body
onto a request with no body at all is never correct.  Every other
caller-set header is resent unchanged on every hop.

The request URL accepts http://, https://, and http+unix:// (Unix
domain socket support is identical across every tier; the
encoded-socket-path form is "http+unix://%2Fpath%2Fto%2Fsock/path",
matching Python's requests-unixsocket convention).  Both a plain
hostname/IPv4 literal
and a bracketed IPv6 literal ("https://[::1]:8443/path") are accepted
for the network forms.  A URL may embed credentials
("http://user:pass@host/path"); they are turned into an
Authorization: Basic ... header automatically unless the request
already sets its own Authorization header (not applicable to
http+unix://, which has no userinfo convention).  A trailing
"#fragment" is recognized and discarded (fragments are never sent to
a server).

Any interim informational (1xx) response the server sends is
transparently discarded, and reading continues on the same
connection for the real response; a caller never sees a 1xx status
as though it were the final answer. RFC 8297 "103 Early Hints" is
the most common real-world example, and may precede an ordinary
response with no req->expect_continue involvement at all. A server
that never stops sending interim responses is not tolerated
indefinitely: after 64 consecutive discarded interim responses on
one hop, chttpclient_do gives up and returns
ccol_http_transfer_aborted, particularly relevant given
request_timeout_ms defaults to 0 (no timeout).

If req->expect_continue is true and the request has a body, only the
request's headers are sent first, followed by up to a one second
wait for the server's response:

  - If the server sends a "100 Continue" interim response, the body
    is sent and the real final response is read normally.
  - If the server sends some OTHER interim 1xx status while waiting
    (e.g. "103 Early Hints" ahead of "100 Continue"), it is
    discarded and the wait continues, still bounded by the same
    one-second budget.
  - If the server answers with a final status directly instead
    (e.g. to reject the request without wanting the body), that
    response is delivered to the caller as-is and the body is never
    sent.
  - If the one second wait elapses with no response at all, the
    body is sent anyway and the final response is read normally
    (the server is assumed not to support the mechanism).

expect_continue has no effect on a request with no body, and is
currently honored only by chttpclient_do and chttpclient_do_streaming
(Tier 1); chttpclient_do_async and everything built on it ignore it.

Once a "100 Continue" interim response has actually been received and
the body written to that connection, a subsequent failure on that
same connection is reported directly and never silently retried
against a fresh connection, even if the connection was reused from
the keep-alive idle pool: the server has already proven it was alive
and accepted the body, so retrying would resend it to an unrelated
second connection and risk a non-idempotent request being processed
twice. See "Connection Pool Behaviour" in README.md for the general
reused-connection retry-once behavior this is the one exception to.

The buffered response body has no size limit by default. Call
chttpclient_set_max_response_body_size to cap it; a response
exceeding that cap is reported as ccol_msg_too_large instead of
being buffered in full.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
cli       Client handle; must not be CHTTPCLI_INVALID.
req       Request to execute; must not be NULL.
resp_out  Output parameter; must not be NULL.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success
       Request completed; *resp_out is valid.

ccol_invalid_args
       Any argument is NULL, cli is CHTTPCLI_INVALID or a stale/
       already-destroyed handle, a request header (or, on a request
       with a body, body.content_type) contains an embedded CR or LF
       byte, a request header is named "Transfer-Encoding", or (on a
       body-carrying request: POST, PUT, or PATCH) a request header
       named "Content-Length" does not exactly match the actual
       body length being sent; see chttp_request_set_header(3).

ccol_not_enough_memory
       Memory allocation failed.

ccol_timed_out
       Request or connect timeout triggered.

ccol_not_permitted
       Client is being destroyed; no new requests are accepted.

ccol_http_invalid_url
       URL is malformed, uses an unsupported scheme (only http://,
       https://, and http+unix:// are supported), has a missing/
       invalid host, port, or userinfo component (including an
       embedded CR or LF byte in the host or in the path/query,
       which would otherwise be carried verbatim onto the wire and
       let it inject extra header lines or a whole smuggled second
       request), or (for http+unix://) names a socket path too long
       to fit sockaddr_un.sun_path.

ccol_http_host_resolution_failed
       DNS resolution failed for the target host (never returned for
       http+unix://, which has no DNS step).

ccol_http_connection_failed
       The connection could not be established (e.g. connection
       refused).

ccol_http_too_many_redirects
       The redirect chain exceeded 50 hops.

ccol_http_tls_handshake_failed
       The TLS handshake failed for a reason other than certificate
       verification.

ccol_http_tls_cert_verification_failed
       The peer certificate or hostname could not be verified.

ccol_http_tls_cert_load_failed
       The configured client certificate, key, or CA bundle path was
       not readable, or ctls failed to load/parse it.

ccol_http_transfer_aborted
       The connection failed mid-transfer, the server sent a
       malformed HTTP/1.1 response, or a streaming chttpcli_write_fn
       returned fewer bytes than it was given.

ccol_msg_too_large
       The response body exceeded
       chttpclient_set_max_response_body_size's configured cap.
       Never returned unless that cap has been set.

ccol_unexpected_failure
       Any other internal failure not covered by the above codes.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttpclient_do_streaming (3),
.BR chttpclient_do_async (3),
.BR chttpclient_set_max_response_body_size (3),
.BR chttp_do (3),
.BR chttpclient_resp_free (3)
